Kernel 23 — Zodiac: Deterministic Schedule Keeper
0. Mandate

Build Zodiac, Pantheon’s deterministic schedule keeper.

Zodiac owns:

What is supposed to happen when.

It does not decide what matters.

It does not interpret natural language.

It does not reason about intent.

It does not replace Father Time, Janus, Apollo, Google Calendar, Apple Calendar, or the operating system.

It receives explicit schedule/obligation declarations, maintains recurrence and due state, reconciles external calendar projections, generates due instances, detects conflicts, and hands consequential work into the existing Pantheon authority path.

Kernel 22 established the mail/intelligence pipeline and prepares temporal proposals.

Kernel 23 makes those proposals durable obligations instead of prose somebody has to remember.

1. Constitutional Status

No constitutional amendment is intended.

Preserve the existing foundational distinction:

Intelligence is not authority.

Models reason. Daemons perform narrow roles. Pantheon authorizes.

Zodiac is deterministic machinery.

It may evaluate explicit rules such as:

every day at 07:00
every hour
after event X
if no reply by Friday
not before Monday
due August 31

It must not interpret:

“I should probably get around to that next week.”

That interpretation belongs to Apollo or another appropriate reasoning component before Zodiac receives anything.

2. Responsibility Boundary

Lock the temporal responsibilities as follows:

Father Time
    What time is it?
    When did something happen?
    Chronology / temporal evidence

Janus
    Has a defined temporal threshold or transition occurred?

Zodiac
    What is supposed to happen when?
    What recurring declaration exists?
    What due instance now exists?
    What obligation is waiting on another event?
    What calendar projection corresponds to it?

The active architecture previously reserved a future recurring-schedule owner. Kernel 23 makes that responsibility real under the name Zodiac.

Do not turn Father Time into a scheduler.

Do not turn Janus into a scheduler.

3. Zodiac Is Not a Reasoning Agent

Do not give Zodiac an LLM.

Do not give Zodiac semantic discretion.

Its inputs should already contain normalized declarations such as:

title
source
schedule type
start
end
timezone
recurrence
deadline
dependency
catch-up policy
projection target
authority state

When meaning is unclear:

Needs You

is a valid deterministic state.

4. Canonical Schedule Truth

Zodiac maintains Pantheon’s canonical internal representation of obligations and recurring work.

External calendars are projections, not the sole source of truth.

Conceptually:

                Apple Calendar
                      ↑
                      |
source → Apollo → Zodiac ←→ calendar projection registry
                      |
                      ↓
                Google Calendar

A Zodiac item may project to:

Apple Calendar;
Google Calendar;
both;
neither.

Do not require every Zodiac schedule to exist in an external calendar.

System jobs such as hourly mail processing may belong only to Zodiac.

5. Calendar Provider Independence

Do not architect Zodiac around Google Calendar.

Do not architect it around Apple Calendar.

Create a provider-neutral calendar projection contract behind Bifrost.

Semantic operations should be capable of representing:

calendar.list
calendar.read
calendar.create
calendar.update
calendar.cancel
calendar.reconcile
calendar.availability

Use whatever actual adapters are available and approved.

Possible implementations may include:

Google Calendar API/MCP;
Apple/iCloud CalDAV;
local calendar bridge;
another provider/plugin.

Do not install/download any new connector without Grant’s explicit approval.

Bifrost’s established rule remains:

Presence is not permission.

6. Apple Calendar Is the Primary Personal Projection

For Grant’s current operating model:

Personal / Family → Apple Calendar
Work              → Google Calendar

Apple Calendar is the primary projection for personal/family life because that is the calendar ecosystem used by Grant’s household.

Google Calendar is primarily the work projection.

Do not encode this as an unchangeable universal assumption.

Make it configurable policy.

7. Projection Classification

Apollo may propose a schedule domain such as:

personal
family
work
cross-domain
unknown

Zodiac applies the configured projection policy.

Initial intended behavior:

personal → Apple
family   → Apple
work     → Google
unknown  → Needs You

A work event outside ordinary work hours that Grant has agreed to attend should receive special attention because it affects personal/family time.

This does not necessarily mean duplicate it automatically.

It means Zodiac should surface the cross-domain consequence and determine whether dual projection is warranted under explicit/learned policy.

8. Dual Projection

A single Zodiac item may project to multiple calendars.

Example:

Work conference
Tuesday 18:30–21:00

Potential result:

Google Work Calendar → work event
Apple Family Calendar → household-impact projection

Do not create two independent obligations.

Both external events should reference one Zodiac item.

Persist each provider’s event identifier separately.

9. External Projection Registry

For each external projection preserve at minimum:

zodiac_item_id
provider
account/calendar
external_event_id
projection_role
created_at
last_synced_at
last_seen_external_revision
status

This enables reconciliation without duplicate creation.

Do not depend on title/time matching alone once an external event ID is known.

10. Human Calendar Edits Are Corrections

If Grant or another authorized human edits a projected calendar event directly in Apple or Google Calendar, treat that as evidence of a human correction, not as provider drift to overwrite automatically.

Zodiac should ingest and reconcile that edit.

General precedence:

human edit > prior Pantheon-generated projection

Do not automatically “repair” the human’s calendar back to what Pantheon originally wrote.

11. Conflicting Human Corrections

If two external projections of the same Zodiac obligation receive incompatible human edits:

Apple: Thursday 2 PM
Google: Thursday 4 PM

Zodiac shall not choose a winner based solely on:

latest timestamp;
provider preference;
alphabetical order;
arbitrary synchronization direction.

State:

Needs You

Preserve both revisions and explain the conflict.

12. Calendar Authority by Event Class

Zodiac may learn explicit deterministic policies such as:

family medical → Apple authoritative
MFPI work meetings → Google authoritative
work events outside work hours → dual visibility / attention

Learning here means:

a human-approved explicit rule is created from observed corrections.

It does not mean opaque statistical self-modification.

The rule must be inspectable and reversible.

13. Schedule Types

Support at minimum:

one-time event
one-time reminder
deadline
recurring event
recurring system job
conditional reminder
follow-up
dependency
date-only obligation
daypart obligation

Do not force all schedules into calendar-event semantics.

14. Exact Times

Support timezone-aware exact times.

Example:

2026-08-19 14:00 America/Los_Angeles

Store canonical timezone information.

Do not rely solely on UTC display.

15. Date-Only Obligations

Support obligations such as:

“Due Friday.”

Do not invent a clock time unless policy supplies one.

Represent:

date = 2026-08-21
time = null
precision = date

Zodiac may later trigger reminders according to explicit notification policy.

16. Dayparts

Support normalized dayparts such as:

morning
afternoon
evening

Where Apollo converts natural language into a representative time, preserve the original precision and wording.

Do not silently pretend:

Friday afternoon

means:

Friday at exactly 3:00 PM

even if 3:00 PM is used operationally as a reminder anchor.

17. Recurrence

Support useful recurrence patterns including:

hourly
daily
weekdays
weekly
every N weeks
monthly
specific weekdays
annual
bounded recurrence
until date
N occurrences

Use a standard recurrence representation where practical.

Do not invent a custom recurrence language if iCalendar RRULE or an equally established format satisfies the contract.

18. Catch-Up Policy

Every recurring schedule owns an explicit catch-up policy.

Do not implement one universal missed-job behavior.

Support at least:

skip

Missed occurrence remains missed.

run_once

After recovery, create one current due instance.

reconcile

Inspect the current source/state and perform enough work to become current.

Examples:

hourly mail intake → reconcile
daily briefing     → configured policy
backup              → run_once
historical reminder → skip or explicit policy

Persist this per schedule.

19. No Replay Avalanche

After Pantheon has been offline:

missed hourly mail × 13 hours

must not automatically become:

13 independent mail runs

when the correct policy is reconciliation.

Generate due work according to the schedule’s catch-up policy.

20. Due Instances

Separate the recurring declaration from individual occurrences.

Example:

Schedule:
Mail intake every hour

Instances:
2026-08-18 09:00
2026-08-18 10:00
2026-08-18 11:00

Each due instance should have its own lifecycle:

pending
due
dispatched
completed
failed
skipped
blocked
Needs You

Do not mutate the recurrence declaration into a pseudo-job-history blob.

21. Idempotence

Each due instance needs a stable identity.

Repeated Zodiac evaluation must not generate duplicate work for the same occurrence.

External calendar projection also must not generate duplicate events merely because reconciliation ran twice.

22. Source Provenance

Every schedule/obligation should retain where it came from.

Examples:

human GUI creation
mail message
transcript
calendar import
Pantheon system declaration
Apollo recommendation
legacy Hermes schedule
configuration migration

Preserve source citations.

23. Request Attestation

When Apollo concludes that Grant requested a scheduled action from a transcript or other source, require a structured attestation packet.

Example:

claim: Grant requested a calendar action
source_id: transcript-2026-08-18
speaker: Grant
citation:
  start: 00:42:17
  end: 00:42:29
quoted_span: "Put that on my calendar for Thursday at two."
confidence: 0.97

normalized:
  action: calendar.create
  title: ...
  start: ...
  timezone: America/Los_Angeles

Apollo supplies the semantic judgment.

A deterministic attestation stage verifies source integrity.

24. Deterministic Attestation

The attestation checker may verify:

source exists;
source fingerprint/version matches;
cited span exists;
quotation matches cited source;
timestamps/line references are valid;
attributed speaker matches available deterministic diarization/speaker metadata;
Apollo did not fabricate or materially alter the quoted text;
normalized temporal values are mechanically consistent where checkable.

The checker does not decide:

Did Grant truly intend this?

That is semantic interpretation.

Do not create another reasoning daemon merely to second-guess Apollo.

25. Elevated Priority for Direct Human Requests

If:

Apollo identifies an explicit Grant request;
the source citation passes deterministic attestation;
confidence is sufficient under policy;

then the resulting Zodiac proposal receives elevated priority as:

person_requested

This does not bypass authority.

It does mean the system should treat the obligation as unusually important and surface ambiguity rather than quietly discarding it.

26. Ambiguous Requests

If Apollo finds:

“Maybe remind me about that sometime Friday.”

preserve:

semantic request: likely
precision: ambiguous
confidence: ...
source wording: ...

Zodiac should normally place this in:

Needs You

if executing the wrong interpretation would matter.

Do not force certainty to improve automation statistics.

27. Needs You

Needs You is a first-class Zodiac state.

Use it for:

ambiguous time/date;
unknown calendar target;
calendar conflict;
conflicting external human corrections;
insufficient authority;
unclear recurrence;
uncertain participant;
consequential schedule interpretation.

This should be visible prominently in Panopticon.

28. Dependencies

Support simple deterministic dependencies.

Examples:

after_event
after_reply
if_no_reply_by
not_before
not_after
after_completion

The natural-language interpretation belongs upstream.

Zodiac stores an explicit condition.

29. Conditional Follow-Up Example

Source:

“If Sarah hasn’t replied by Friday, remind me.”

Apollo produces:

type: conditional_reminder
condition:
  kind: no_reply
  correspondence_ref: ...
deadline:
  date: ...
action:
  kind: reminder

Zodiac evaluates the deterministic condition.

It does not reinterpret the original sentence every hour.

30. Dependency Resolution

A dependency may reference another Pantheon entity using stable identifiers.

Avoid brittle text comparisons such as:

if subject contains "Sarah"

when a stable thread/person/work identifier exists.

If the referenced source cannot be evaluated reliably:

Needs You

rather than guessing.

31. Calendar Availability

Where approved adapters expose availability/free-busy information, Zodiac may use it as deterministic evidence for conflict detection.

It may determine:

candidate time conflicts
candidate time is free

It must not decide that a conflicting event is expendable.

32. Conflict Detection

Detect:

event overlaps
deadline collision where policy defines one
cross-domain work/personal collision
dual-projection divergence

Do not auto-resolve consequential conflicts.

Use:

Needs You

unless a deterministic human-approved policy already resolves that exact class.

33. Work Outside Work Hours

Create a configurable rule for work events that fall outside ordinary work hours.

When Grant has agreed to attend such an event:

elevate attention;
recognize personal/family impact;
consider Apple-family visibility according to projection policy;
surface the event rather than letting it remain silently work-only.

Do not assume all after-hours work events belong on both calendars.

Learn explicit rules from human decisions.

34. External Calendar Ingestion

Zodiac should ingest relevant external calendar state from both approved providers.

At minimum retrieve enough to:

reconcile Pantheon-created events;
detect human edits;
detect external additions;
detect conflicts;
preserve external event IDs;
determine projection health.

Do not ingest unnecessary historical calendar content merely because the API permits it.

35. External Events Not Created by Pantheon

An event created manually in Apple or Google Calendar may become a Zodiac obligation.

Mark provenance:

external_human_created

Do not require Apollo analysis merely to preserve an explicit structured calendar event.

Apollo may be involved later if semantic interpretation is useful.

36. Calendar Deletion

If a human deletes a Pantheon-projected event externally:

do not recreate it automatically on the next sync.

Treat deletion as a human correction unless evidence strongly indicates provider failure.

Update Zodiac state accordingly or place the reconciliation into Needs You if another projection conflicts.

37. Calendar Notifications

External calendar projection should make useful use of the provider/device notification ecosystem.

For Apple Calendar, preserve/configure reminders so household devices behave normally.

For Google Calendar, preserve/configure reminders appropriate to work.

Default to the target calendar’s established reminder behavior unless:

the source explicitly specifies a reminder;
a human-approved Zodiac rule specifies another behavior.

Do not create duplicate Pantheon notifications for every external calendar reminder.

38. Pantheon Notifications

Zodiac may also route important schedule state through Pantheon’s existing notification system.

Examples:

conflict;
Needs You;
failed projection;
deadline approaching with unresolved approval;
schedule processing failure.

Zodiac does not create a new notification channel.

39. System Schedules

Zodiac owns recurring Pantheon application-level schedules.

Initial migration candidates:

hourly mail processing
daily 7:00 AM Situation Brief
daily transcript processing
notification/reminder evaluation

Do not blindly migrate OS lifecycle work.

